# Boy Scout rule

**Each time you create or modify a file, leave it better than you found it**, and **record / raise a code-quality score in that file’s header**.

## Quality score (required on every create / edit)

At the end of your change to a file, ensure its header includes a **Quality** line:

```text
Quality: N/10 — short note (what holds the score back, or why it’s strong)
```

### Where it lives

| Kind | Placement |
|------|-----------|
| JS / CSS / Python | In the top module comment, after Intent / Architecture |
| HTML | HTML comment near the top of `<body>` or after `<!DOCTYPE>` |
| Cursor `.mdc` rules | HTML comment immediately after the YAML frontmatter |

Example (JS):

```js
/**
 * Intent: …
 * Architecture: …
 * Quality: 7/10 — clear facade; createChat still a bit long
 */
```

### Scale (be honest)

| Score | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| 1–3 | Hard to follow, mixed concerns, or dangerous edges |
| 4–5 | Works but messy / oversized / weak boundaries |
| 6–7 | Solid and readable; a few concrete smells remain |
| 8–9 | Clean, focused, easy to change; minor nits only |
| 10 | Reserved — essentially nothing useful left to improve in-file |

If the file had **no** score yet, **add one** (first estimate after your edit).  
If it already had a score, **try to improve it by ≥1** via a real local cleanup in the same edit (not by inflating the number). If you cannot raise it this pass, keep the score and update the note with what still blocks a higher mark.

## Boy Scout cleanup (required)

On every touched file, also do **one** nearby improvement, for example:

- Remove dead code / obsolete comments
- Fix a misleading name next to your change
- Refresh Intent / Architecture if the role shifted
- Dedupe a helper you just had to touch
- Drop an unused import
- Split or extract only if it clearly raises the Quality score without widening scope

## Limits

- Only files required for the task — no repo-wide score campaigns.
- Do not bump the score without a real improvement.
- Do not expand into unrelated refactors or formatting-only diffs.
- Prefer the smallest change that raises clarity or the score.

## ❌ Avoid

- “I’ll score / clean it later” on a file you already edited
- Raising `Quality` while leaving known dead code in that file
- Deleting tests or weakening contracts to “simplify”
